 & Medicaid Services relating to \"Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act; Marketplace Integrity and Affordability\".","introducedDate":"2025-09-17","latestActionDate":"2025-09-17","latestActionText":"Referred to the Committee on Energy and Commerce, and in addition to the Committee on Ways and Means, for a period to be subsequently determined by the Speaker, in each case for consideration of such provisions as fall within the jurisdiction of the committee concerned.","policyArea":"Health","subjects":["Administrative law and regulatory procedures","Border security and unlawful immigration","Congressional oversight","Department of Health and Human Services","Drug therapy","Health care costs and insurance","Health care coverage and access","Immigrant health and welfare","Sex, gender, sexual orientation discrimination","Surgery and anesthesia"],"sponsorBioguideId":"S001223","cosponsorCount":34,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-joint-resolution/123","industries":[{"industry":"insurance","confidence":0.95,"rationale":"The bill would reverse requirements affecting health insurers' enrollment processes, eligibility verification obligations, and coverage mandates for essential health benefits."},{"industry":"health-providers","confidence":0.6,"rationale":"Changes to marketplace enrollment periods and coverage of certain procedures would affect patient volume and reimbursement for hospitals and physician groups."}],"plainSummary":"This resolution would block a federal rule issued in June 2025 that changes how people enroll in health insurance through the Affordable Care Act marketplaces. The rule sets new enrollment deadlines, requires more identity checks for certain applicants, bars people with DACA status from using the marketplaces, and prevents insurers from covering certain medical procedures as essential benefits. If passed, the resolution would nullify these changes and leave prior rules in place.","classi